-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Each kiosk maintains an embedded SQLite index built from the active signed snapshot. The index is private storage: callers cannot issue SQL or depend on its schema, file location, or lifecycle.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+The importer verifies the snapshot manifest and content signature using a bundled public key before trusting record data. It rejects unsupported schema versions, malformed or incomplete records, and serial numbers that collide after lookup normalization.
+
+For an accepted snapshot, the importer creates a new SQLite database in a staging location on the same filesystem as the active index. It streams the newline-delimited records into a transaction, stores the snapshot date as index metadata, and creates a unique index over the normalized serial-number key. The source record's serial number remains available as record data; the normalized key is used only for matching and uniqueness.
+
+The importer checks the completed database before activation, then atomically replaces the active-index reference. Lookups continue against the previous database until that replacement and use either the complete old index or the complete new index. A validation, indexing, storage, or activation failure removes the staged database, leaves the previous index active, and reports the failed import stage without exposing record contents.
+
+On startup, the registry opens the active index and verifies its schema version and required snapshot metadata before serving lookups. Staging databases left by an interrupted import are never treated as active and may be removed during startup recovery.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before querying the indexed normalized key. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. A lookup is a read-only, parameterized equality query that returns at most one record. Return the active index's snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+The registry keeps the active database connection behind `findBySerial`; certificate preparation does not manage connection state or retry storage operations. Imports and lookups are coordinated inside the registry so an activation cannot expose a closed, staged, or partially built database to a caller.
+
+## Resource Bounds
+
+Index construction streams records rather than loading the snapshot into memory. SQLite owns the on-disk search structure and may use a bounded page cache; the registry does not build a second in-memory copy of the 90,000-record dataset. Exact-match lookup uses the unique normalized-serial index so performance does not depend on a full table scan.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Per-kiosk embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps every lookup local and predictable inside the isolated production area. It accepts per-kiosk validation and index rebuild work while avoiding network availability, service timeout, credential, and recurring-cost concerns. |
+| Index activation | Build a separate staged database and atomically replace the active reference | Rebuild the active database in place | Preserves the last valid snapshot and prevents lookups from observing partial imports. |
+| Serial lookup key | Unique normalized key alongside source record data | Case-insensitive query over the source value | Makes lookup and duplicate detection use the same normalization while preserving the supplied serial number for display. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+- Define registry behavior before the kiosk has completed its first valid snapshot import.
+- Define whether imports can be requested concurrently or whether a second request is rejected while one is running.
+- Set an explicit local storage headroom requirement for the active index, staged index, and source snapshot during import.
